WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:08.736
FAYE MOSS: Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History , the only talk show that features history's

2
00:00:08.737 --> 00:00:14.866
biggest stars. I'm Faye Moss. Today's guest is a ruler from the

3
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:21.213
ancient world. Nearly everybody knows her name, but few people know

4
00:00:21.214 --> 00:00:48.567
her secrets. Maybe I can get her to reveal some of them! Straight from history, here is the last queen of Egypt,

5
00:00:48.568 --> 00:00:52.731
Cleopatra! CLEOPATRA: Thank you, Faye, dear. It's lovely to

6
00:00:52.732 --> 00:00:56.825
be here. FAYE MOSS: We're so thrilled to have

7
00:00:56.826 --> 00:01:07.987
you, Your Majesty. You were born in 69 BC, an Egyptian princess in the Ptolemaic dynasty that had ruled Egypt

8
00:01:07.988 --> 00:01:12.812
for 275 years. But your family was actually Greek, not

9
00:01:12.813 --> 00:01:19.150
Egyptian, right? CLEOPATRA: It's true that my ancestors were Greek, but in my heart,

10
00:01:19.151 --> 00:01:24.725
I was Egyptian. I spoke the language—in fact, I was the only ruler in my family who

11
00:01:24.726 --> 00:01:30.310
ever did. I also dressed as an Egyptian and took part in Egyptian rituals

12
00:01:30.311 --> 00:01:35.716
and customs. I felt great pride in my kingdom, even though we were going through hard

13
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:37.753
times. FAYE MOSS:

14
00:01:37.754 --> 00:01:42.208
What was happening? CLEOPATRA: The people were

15
00:01:42.209 --> 00:01:45.985
unhappy, and understandably so. There were food shortages, and the

16
00:01:45.986 --> 00:01:52.516
economy was in trouble. My father owed a massive debt to Rome, and Rome itself was an ongoing

17
00:01:52.517 --> 00:02:01.865
and serious problem. Rome had been expanding its power throughout the region, and under previous pharaohs, its influence in Egypt had grown, threatening

18
00:02:01.866 --> 00:02:06.330
our independence. FAYE MOSS: Your relationship with Rome

19
00:02:06.331 --> 00:02:16.098
was complicated, wasn't it? When you were only 18 years old, your father died, and you became queen, ruling alongside your younger

20
00:02:16.099 --> 00:02:19.797
brother Ptolemy XIII. Rome helped you solve

21
00:02:19.798 --> 00:02:30.718
an early problem. CLEOPATRA: Yes, my father had left his kingdom to be ruled jointly by us, but Ptolemy, who was only ten by the way, refused to acknowledge

22
00:02:30.719 --> 00:02:35.838
me as co-ruler. Siblings! I was forced to flee the palace to save

23
00:02:35.839 --> 00:02:43.582
my life. Meanwhile, Julius Caesar, who was then the ruler of Rome, was staying at the royal palace

24
00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:48.872
in Alexandria. I knew that if I could get Caesar's support, I could reclaim my

25
00:02:48.873 --> 00:02:53.407
place. So I figured out a way to sneak into the palace to talk to

26
00:02:53.408 --> 00:02:59.524
him. FAYE MOSS: There's a story that you wrapped yourself in a carpet that was gifted

27
00:02:59.525 --> 00:03:06.491
to Caesar. When the carpet was unrolled, you popped out! Or maybe it was some type

28
00:03:06.492 --> 00:03:13.725
of sack? CLEOPATRA: They still tell that story? How I managed to arrange an audience with Caesar is my

29
00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:18.869
little secret. In any case, it worked, and Caesar reinstated me

30
00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.954
as co-ruler. This didn't deter Ptolemy from wanting to get rid of

31
00:03:22.955 --> 00:03:33.610
me. Eventually he joined forces with my younger sister, Arsinoe, and attempted to depose me once again, but things didn't turn out well for

32
00:03:33.611 --> 00:03:39.208
him. He drowned in the Nile after his army was defeated by mine and Caesar's.

33
00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:46.411
From then I had to co-rule with my youngest brother, but at least Caesar secured my position. FAYE

34
00:03:46.412 --> 00:03:49.918
MOSS: It sounds like he helped you a

35
00:03:49.919 --> 00:03:54.049
lot. CLEOPATRA: We helped each other and worked

36
00:03:54.050 --> 00:04:00.628
well together. Rome needed Egyptian grain and depended on our economy to support its expanding

37
00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:05.596
territory. And I persuaded Caesar to return some Egyptian lands that Rome had

38
00:04:05.597 --> 00:04:09.619
taken. This allowed me to reduce taxes and improve our

39
00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:17.909
economy. Caesar also provided Roman legions to protect Alexandria, freeing up my money and troops for other uses.

40
00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:31.313
You may have heard that we had a personal relationship too; we had a son named Caesarion—the heir to Egypt, and, I had hoped, to Rome as well. FAYE

41
00:04:31.314 --> 00:04:38.291
MOSS: Why don't we talk a little bit more about your reign? What were your goals as ruler?

42
00:04:38.292 --> 00:04:44.542
CLEOPATRA: More than anything, I wanted to bring back Egypt's glory and

43
00:04:44.543 --> 00:04:54.945
independence. During my reign I restored the kingdom to the size and grandeur it had known a thousand years before, and Egypt became a world power once again.

44
00:04:54.946 --> 00:04:59.714
But I also wanted to be loved by my subjects.

45
00:04:59.715 --> 00:05:07.595
So I ended taxation on starving farmers and released grain from the royal granaries so my people could eat. I

46
00:05:07.596 --> 00:05:13.087
brought scholars to Alexandria to study and work in our great library. And

47
00:05:13.088 --> 00:05:18.775
I allowed Jews to settle in Egypt and built a synagogue in Alexandria. As my

48
00:05:18.776 --> 00:05:28.888
kingdom was made up of people who spoke many languages, I learned further languages so that I could communicate with all my people. The kingdom prospered,

49
00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:33.533
and my people worshipped me. FAYE MOSS: How

50
00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:36.469
did Rome react to this? CLEOPATRA:

51
00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:44.259
Relations with Rome were fine under Caesar, but that ended when Caesar was murdered in 44 BC. Caesar

52
00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:58.665
never did name Caesarion as his heir, but I made him my co-ruler after . . . Well, let's just say that I took steps to get rid of my brother. You might

53
00:05:58.666 --> 00:06:03.582
think it harsh, but it was the way my family did things. FAYE MOSS:

54
00:06:03.583 --> 00:06:09.928
Yikes! I don't want to get on your bad side, so let's move on. What

55
00:06:09.929 --> 00:06:13.888
happened to Rome after Caesar's death? CLEOPATRA:

56
00:06:13.889 --> 00:06:21.688
Rome was divided. Caesar's nephew, commonly called Octavian, ruled the western half. Mark

57
00:06:21.689 --> 00:06:27.773
Antony, who ruled the eastern half, called me to his side to help him. Antony

58
00:06:27.774 --> 00:06:32.511
was a wise leader and skilled politician. FAYE

59
00:06:32.512 --> 00:06:37.088
MOSS: And you had a relationship with him as well.

60
00:06:37.089 --> 00:06:46.936
CLEOPATRA: We had three children together, but more importantly for this discussion, we became joint rulers of our two very powerful

61
00:06:46.937 --> 00:06:54.181
kingdoms. Unfortunately Octavian wanted to be rid of us. He hated—and feared—me!

62
00:06:54.182 --> 00:07:02.685
My son Caesarion had a strong claim to Octavian's throne, and both Antony and I declared him heir.

63
00:07:02.686 --> 00:07:07.733
Octavian did not feel safe as long as we lived. So

64
00:07:07.734 --> 00:07:13.603
Octavian declared war on me and sent his troops after us. His

65
00:07:13.604 --> 00:07:22.532
hatred was personal as well. Antony was married to Octavian's sister before he left her to support me. FAYE MOSS:

66
00:07:22.533 --> 00:07:25.339
So what did you do?

67
00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:32.786
CLEOPATRA: We fled and ordered ships and soldiers to gather at Actium in Greece to defend our kingdoms.

68
00:07:32.787 --> 00:07:36.869
It wasn't long before Octavian's fleet arrived . . .

69
00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:43.106
FAYE MOSS: I can finish the story, Your Majesty. We're so honored that you joined us

70
00:07:43.107 --> 00:07:46.389
today. We'll let you rejoin history

71
00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:50.919
now. CLEOPATRA: Thank

72
00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:59.783
you, Faye. FAYE MOSS: Cleopatra and Mark Antony's forces were defeated by Octavian at the Battle of

73
00:07:59.784 --> 00:08:07.161
Actium in 31 BC. Antony, after receiving false word that Cleopatra was dead, ended his own

74
00:08:07.162 --> 00:08:12.466
life in despair. Octavian was determined to bring Cleopatra to

75
00:08:12.467 --> 00:08:22.440
Rome in chains. To avoid this fate, Cleopatra took her own life, ending her 22-year reign and briefly leaving Caesarion

76
00:08:22.441 --> 00:08:32.713
as pharaoh. Octavian, now Augustus Caesar, the first Roman emperor, swiftly removed Caesarion and made Egypt part of the Roman

77
00:08:32.714 --> 00:08:42.855
Empire. Cleopatra's gift for governing and her relationships with two powerful Roman politicians made her an unforgettable

78
00:08:42.856 --> 00:08:49.473
pharaoh. Her story, with its tragic ending, has inspired many retellings over the centuries.

79
00:08:49.474 --> 00:08:57.938
I hope you enjoyed today's show. See you next time!

